199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (Every culture has its own traditions, beliefs, and advice that gets passed down from one generation to another. In Pakistan, fitness advice is often shared just as freely as family recipes. One person says drinking hot water melts fat, another insists eating after 8 p.m. causes weight gain, while someone else believes lifting weights is only meant for men. Because these ideas are repeated so often, they begin to sound like facts.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (Unfortunately, many of these beliefs have little or no scientific evidence behind them. Some are harmless, while others stop people from adopting healthier lifestyles. A beginner who believes they need expensive supplements to exercise may never start. A woman who thinks strength training will make her "look like a man" may miss out on one of the healthiest forms of exercise.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (This article explores some of the most common fitness myths found in Pakistani households and explains the truth behind them. The aim is not to criticize traditions but to encourage readers to question information before accepting it. Understanding the facts allows us to make better choices for our bodies while still respecting our culture.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
